<ul><li>The printed column header of the infinite contrast is the symbol of infinity.</li><li>Tables 3.1 to 3.3 are complete; Table A.1 goes to the degree 50 in the book, of which the degrees 2 to 8 are transcribed.</li><li>Table conductivity_sc: Table 3.1, p. 67. Effective conductivity of the simple cubic array of spheres, over that of the matrix.</li><li>Table conductivity_bcc: Table 3.2, p. 67. Effective conductivity of the body-centered cubic array of spheres, over that of the matrix.</li><li>Table conductivity_fcc: Table 3.3, p. 67. Effective conductivity of the face-centered cubic array of spheres, over that of the matrix.</li><li>Table lattice_sums_sc: Table A.1, p. 465. First rows of the lattice sums of the simple cubic lattice of unit period, divided by N_ts = sqrt((t-s)!(t+s)!): harmonic Y_ts(0) and biharmonic Z_ts(0). The harmonic sum of degree 2, conditionally convergent, is given in the convention of the book.</li><li>In the conductivity tables the columns contrast_0 to contrast_infinity are the conductivity of the spheres over that of the matrix; contrast_infinity stands for perfectly conducting spheres.</li><li>Table stiffness_sc: Table 5.1, p. 149. Effective stiffness of the simple cubic array of spheres, nu0 = nu1 = 0.3 (Section 5.7, p. 145): C1122/(2 mu0), C1111/(2 mu0) and C1212/mu0, the contrast being mu1/mu0.</li><li>Table stiffness_bcc: Table 5.2, p. 150. Effective stiffness of the body-centered cubic array of spheres, nu0 = nu1 = 0.3 (Section 5.7, p. 145): C1122/(2 mu0), C1111/(2 mu0) and C1212/mu0, the contrast being mu1/mu0.</li><li>Table stiffness_fcc: Table 5.3, p. 150. Effective stiffness of the face-centered cubic array of spheres, nu0 = nu1 = 0.3 (Section 5.7, p. 145): C1122/(2 mu0), C1111/(2 mu0) and C1212/mu0, the contrast being mu1/mu0.</li><li>Tables stiffness_bcc: the entries 2.6782 (c = 0.4, C1212, contrast 1000) and 6.2030 (c = 0.6, same column) are printed with four decimals in the book; they are transcribed as printed.</li><li>Table stiffness_spheroids: Table 7.6, p. 244, columns tmax = 15. C3333/mu0 of an orthogonal array of spheroids of semi-axes (1, 1, aspect_ratio), periods proportional to the semi-axes, nu0 = nu1 = 0.3, the contrast being mu1/mu0 (0 for cavities); the columns c_0.1 to c_0.5 are the volume fraction.</li><li>Table conductivity_cracks_so: Table 6.3, p. 197. Effective conductivity lambda*33/lambda0 of a solid with insulating penny-shaped cracks of unit radius and normal e3 on the orthogonal lattice of periods (a1, a1, a3), one crack per cell; the key a3_over_a1_1_2 is a3/a1 = 1/2, and a3_over_a1_0 the limit a3/a1 -&gt; 0, which no computation at finite a3 reaches. The entry a1 = 4, a3/a1 = 1/2 is printed 0.932; the multipole expansion gives 0.923, the same digits transposed, in line with the body-centered array of the same crack density (0.919, Table 6.4): it is transcribed as printed.</li><li>Table conductivity_cracks_bc: Table 6.4, p. 198. The same with two cracks per cell, at the origin and at (a1/2, a1/2, a3/2) (body-centered array). The entries 0.0045 and 0.00005 are printed 0.45 · 10^-2 and 0.5 · 10^-4.</li><li>Tables young_cracks_so, shear_cracks_so, young_cracks_bc, shear_cracks_bc: Tables 7.7 to 7.10, pp. 245-246. E*33/E0 = 1/(E0 D*3333) and mu*13/mu0 = 1/(4 mu0 D*1313), D* the effective compliance, of a solid of Poisson ratio nu0 = 0.25 with traction-free penny-shaped cracks of unit radius and normal e3 on the lattice of periods (a, a, a3); the key a_over_a3_1_2 is a/a3 = 1/2. SO: one crack per cell; BC: two, at the origin and at (a/2, a/2, a3/2).</li></ul>
